---
// Safe to edit. The prose below is the public accessibility statement; rewrite
// any paragraph in place. Bump lastUpdated when meaningful changes ship. Keep
// it honest: only claim what the site actually does.
/* ============================================================================
   /accessibility | Accessibility statement
   ============================================================================
   The studio sells accessible work (WCAG AA, 100 Lighthouse a11y), so this
   page is the public version of that commitment: the standard targeted, what
   it means in practice, how it's checked, where it deliberately stops short,
   and how to report a problem. The honesty (the "where it stops short" and
   "found a problem" sections) is what makes it a real statement rather than a
   marketing boast.

   Layout: DocumentLayout (overhaul 2.9), shared with /privacy and /colophon:
   H1, "Last updated", the sticky "On this page" list at lg and one prose
   measure. `sections` below is the single source for the list and the ids.
   ============================================================================ */

import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';

const lastUpdated = 'June 27, 2026';

const sections = [
  { id: 'the-standard', label: 'The standard' },
  { id: 'in-practice', label: 'In practice' },
  { id: 'how-its-checked', label: 'How it is checked' },
  { id: 'where-it-stops', label: 'Where it stops short' },
  { id: 'report', label: 'Found a problem?' },
];

// The practical commitments, as a plain list.
const practices = [
  'Everything you can do with a mouse, you can do with a keyboard. The focus outline is always visible, and a "Skip to main content" link is the first thing you reach.',
  'Real landmarks and a clean heading order, so a screen reader can move through a page by region and by heading instead of reading it all top to bottom.',
  'Every form field has a visible label, uses the right input type, and announces its own errors rather than signalling them with color alone.',
  'Text is chosen to clear the AA contrast bar against its background in both the light and the dark theme. Images that carry meaning have descriptions; purely decorative ones are hidden from assistive tech.',
  'If your device is set to reduce motion, the animations and the smooth scrolling switch off automatically. Anything that moves on its own, like the reel of client sites on the homepage, also stops with the Pause motion button at the bottom of every page, and the site remembers that choice on your device.',
];
---

<DocumentLayout
  title="Accessibility statement | Nixon Creative Studio"
  description="How I build and test this site for keyboard and screen-reader users, the standard it aims for (WCAG 2.1 AA), and how to tell me about a problem."
  tag="Accessibility"
  lead="Built to be used"
  heavy="by everyone."
  updated={lastUpdated}
  sections={sections}
  headingId="accessibility-heading"
>
  <p slot="intro">
    I build this site toward WCAG 2.1 AA, in both the light and the dark theme, and I check it on
    every change. No website is ever perfectly accessible, so if anything is hard to use with a
    keyboard or a screen reader, tell me and I'll fix it: <a href={site.emailHref}>{site.email}</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="the-standard">The standard</h2>
  <p>
    The goal is WCAG 2.1 AA, the same standard most public bodies and larger organizations are held
    to, and it applies in both the light and the dark theme. Each page is built to score 100 on
    Lighthouse's accessibility audit, and an automated check runs on every change so that score does
    not quietly slip. That automated score is a useful floor, not a certificate: it catches the
    machine-checkable issues, which are only one part of the picture.
  </p>

  <h2 id="in-practice">In practice</h2>
  <p>A few of the things that means on this site:</p>
  <ul>
    {practices.map((item) => (
      <li>{item}</li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <h2 id="how-its-checked">How it is checked</h2>
  <p>
    The accessibility score is a hard gate: a change cannot ship if it drops a page below 100. On
    top of that, the pages are run through{' '}
    <a href="https://github.com/dequelabs/axe-core" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
      axe-core
    </a>{' '}
    (a widely used accessibility testing engine) in both themes, and structural changes get a manual
    keyboard pass from the address bar through every control. Automated tools like these only cover
    part of WCAG; the rest is judgment, whether the alt text is actually useful, whether the reading
    order makes sense, so I treat the tooling as a safety net under the hands-on work, not a
    stand-in for it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="where-it-stops">Where it stops short</h2>
  <p>
    I aim for AA, not the stricter AAA, on purpose, in line with the W3C's own guidance that AAA is
    not meant as a blanket requirement for a whole site. A couple of AAA rules pull against a
    brand-led design: the deepest brand-blue text sits just under the 7:1 enhanced-contrast bar, for
    one. So the work targets AA and stops there by choice, not by oversight.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is a line about polish, not about whether the site works. If a specific thing is genuinely
    hard for you to use, that is a bug, and the next section is how to flag it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="report">Found a problem?</h2>
  <p>
    If a page is hard to read, hard to get through, or breaks with the assistive technology you rely
    on, email <a href={site.emailHref}>{site.email}</a> and tell me what happened and what you were
    using. It reaches a real person, me, and I treat accessibility problems as real problems to fix,
    not as nice-to-haves.
  </p>
</DocumentLayout>
